          '• Water Inlet Solenoid Valve Sticking Open: The water inlet solenoid valve is an electromechanical valve that opens when energized to allow fresh water in and snaps shut when the cycle begins. If mineral debris or sand flakes lodge in the valve diaphragm, or if the magnetic coil mechanically jams, the valve will weep or stay stuck wide open. Water continuously pours into the sump, overflowing the standpipe drain continuously even when the machine is switched off.',
          '• Limescale-Coated Water Level Probes: Modern machines (such as Manitowoc and Scotsman) utilize acoustic thickness sensors or electronic conductivity probes to detect when the water trough is full. When hard water mineral scale coats these metal probe tips, the sensor cannot detect water conductivity. The computer board assumes the trough is empty and continues pumping water in until it spills over the plastic reservoir edges.',
          '• Excessive Municipal Water Line Pressure: Most commercial ice makers require incoming water pressure regulated between 20 and 80 PSI. If municipal pressure surges above 90 PSI, incoming water velocity can overpower the internal float valve or cause splashing over internal splash baffles during fill cycles. Installing an inline water pressure regulator set to 45-50 PSI prevents hydraulic splashing.',
