          'An evaporator freeze-up occurs when ice forms on the freezing plate but fails to release during the hot-gas defrost harvest cycle. In subsequent freeze cycles, new layers of water freeze on top of the unharvested slab, forming an enormous block of ice that expands outward like an iceberg.',
          'As this massive ice slab swells beyond the bounds of the evaporator grid, circulating water can no longer cascade down the plate into the collection sump. Instead, water deflects off the bulging ice face, spraying outside the water curtain, hitting internal side panels, and draining straight through cabinet louvers onto the floor.',
          'Root Causes of Freeze-Ups:',
          '• Scaled or Pitted Evaporator Grids: Minerals bond the ice cubes physically to the metal surface, preventing the harvest defrost cycle from dropping the slab.',
          '• Faulty Harvest Gas Valve or Defrost Solenoid: If the hot-gas bypass valve fails to open, hot refrigerant vapor never warms the evaporator plate, and harvest never occurs.',
